<Base title="Runtime-programmable kernel for robots" description="VolnLabs is building AxiomOS, a runtime-programmable kernel for robots. Robots should be able to learn new behavior without anyone losing control of the hardware." bgColor="#000">
  <div class="min">
    <main>
      <a href="/research#papers" class="pill quiet"><span class="dot"></span>2 papers accepted at NeurIPS 2026 →</a>

      <h1>Robots should be able to learn new behavior without anyone losing control of the hardware<span class="accent">.</span></h1>

      <p>VolnLabs is building AxiomOS, a runtime-programmable kernel for robots.</p>

      <h2 id="why">Why</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p><strong>Robots are starting to change themselves.</strong> Developers ship new controllers, policies learn, and AI systems propose new tasks. More and more, these changes reach a robot while it is running.</p>
        <p><strong>Nothing decides what that new code may do.</strong> Today a new behavior either gets full authority over the motors or the whole system gets revalidated. One is unsafe, the other is slow.</p>
        <p><strong>Physical actions can’t be rolled back.</strong> Software can undo a bad update. A robot arm can’t undo a motion it already made. Authority has to be settled before anything moves.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="what">What</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p><strong>Admit new behavior.</strong> A developer or an AI system proposes a new controller, policy or task. AxiomOS checks it and governs how it becomes active, including replacement while the system is running.</p>
        <p><strong>Bound its authority.</strong> Every behavior gets defined permissions and limits: which actuators it can use, under what conditions, and when it must stop. Real-time execution, emergency stops, and guarantees that hold on actual hardware.</p>
        <p><strong>Keep every update recoverable.</strong> Behaviors are versioned and activation is controlled. A rejected update leaves the working state intact. Audit records show what ran and what it was allowed to control.</p>
        <p><strong>Fit the existing stack.</strong> Higher-level intelligence on Linux and NVIDIA-based systems proposes actions. AxiomOS governs execution near the hardware.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="next">Where this goes</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p>VolnLabs is the company. AxiomOS is the core technology. The bigger ambition is adaptable industrial systems, and eventually robots building robots.</p>
        <p><a href="mailto:contact@volnlabs.com" style="font-weight:700;">If you build robots and want them to learn safely, talk to us.</a></p>
      </div>

      <footer>
        <div class="brand">
